{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_whatsnext","fact_refs":[5],"question":"If someone needs help with their specific Django setup rather than reporting a documentation issue, what should they use instead of the ticket system?","answer":"The Django Forum or the Django Discord server."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_whatsnext","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What tool does Django use to convert its documentation from plain text into HTML?","answer":"Sphinx."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The blog post 'The Plan for React 18' was published on June 8, 2021.","source_span":"June 8, 2021 by"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React team started work on React 18, which will be the next major version.","source_span":"We’ve started work on the React 18 release, which will be our next major version."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React team created a Working Group to prepare the community for gradual adoption of new features in React 18.","source_span":"We’ve created a Working Group to prepare the community for gradual adoption of new features in React 18."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React team published a React 18 Alpha so that library authors can try it and provide feedback.","source_span":"We’ve published a React 18 Alpha so that library authors can try it and provide feedback."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"React 18 will include automatic batching, a new startTransition API, and a new streaming server renderer with built-in support for React.lazy.","source_span":"React 18 will include out-of-the-box improvements (like automatic batching), new APIs (like `startTransition`), and a new streaming server renderer with built-in support for `React.lazy`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"Concurrent rendering is a new opt-in mechanism that lets React prepare multiple versions of the UI at the same time.","source_span":"It’s called “concurrent rendering” and it lets React prepare multiple versions of the UI at the same time."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"Concurrent rendering will only be enabled for updates triggered by one of the new features, allowing adoption of React 18 without rewrites.","source_span":"concurrent rendering will only be enabled for updates triggered by one of the new features. In practice, this means you will be able to adopt React 18 without rewrites and try the new features at your own pace."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"Users can upgrade to React 18 with minimal or no changes to their application code, with effort comparable to a typical major React release, and many are expected to upgrade within a single afternoon.","source_span":"You can upgrade to React 18 with minimal or no changes to your application code, with a level of effort comparable to a typical major React release...we expect that many users will be able to upgrade within a single afternoon."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React team successfully shipped concurrent features to tens of thousands of components at Facebook.","source_span":"We successfully shipped concurrent features to tens of thousands of components at Facebook"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React 18 Working Group is hosted on GitHub Discussions and available for the public to read, though only invited members can create or comment on threads.","source_span":"The Working Group is hosted on GitHub Discussions and is available for the public to read....only invited members will be allowed to create or comment on threads. However, the threads are fully visible to the public"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"New React 18 alphas are regularly published to npm using the @alpha tag, built from the most recent commit to the main repo; a merged feature or bugfix appears in an alpha the following weekday.","source_span":"New alphas are regularly published to npm using the `@alpha` tag. These releases are built using the most recent commit to our main repo. When a feature or bugfix is merged, it will appear in an alpha the following weekday."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"Alpha releases of React 18 are not recommended for user-facing, production applications.","source_span":"alpha releases are not recommended for user-facing, production applications"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The projected React 18 release timeline is: Library Alpha available today, Public Beta at least several months later, Release Candidate at least several weeks after Beta, and General Availability at least several weeks after RC.","source_span":"Library Alpha: Available today\n* Public Beta: At least several months\n* Release Candidate (RC): At least several weeks after Beta\n* General Availability: At least several weeks after RC"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[0],"question":"When was 'The Plan for React 18' blog post published?","answer":"June 8, 2021."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[1,2,3],"question":"What three updates did the React team share regarding React 18 in this post?","answer":"They started work on the React 18 release (the next major version), created a Working Group to prepare the community for gradual adoption of new features, and published a React 18 Alpha for library authors to try and give feedback."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is 'concurrent rendering' in React 18?","answer":"A new opt-in mechanism that lets React prepare multiple versions of the UI at the same time."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Where is the React 18 Working Group hosted, and who is allowed to post there?","answer":"It's hosted on GitHub Discussions; only invited members can create or comment on threads, but the threads are fully visible to the public."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[10],"question":"How are React 18 alpha releases published to npm, and when does a merged fix appear in one?","answer":"They're regularly published to npm using the @alpha tag, built from the most recent commit to the main repo; a merged feature or bugfix appears in an alpha the following weekday."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[12],"question":"What is the projected release timeline for React 18 after the Alpha?","answer":"Public Beta at least several months out, Release Candidate at least several weeks after Beta, and General Availability at least several weeks after the RC."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How much effort does the React team expect upgrading to React 18 will take for most applications?","answer":"Minimal or no changes to application code, with effort comparable to a typical major React release; many users are expected to upgrade within a single afternoon."}
